  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 148 AM Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Dry today, cool tonight.\n\nA dry cold front was located over southern VA this morning, \nidentified not only by a southward progressing pre-frontal stratocu \ndeck, but also by decreased dewpoints in the 40s. Ahead of the front \nacross central NC, lingering mid/upper 50s dewpoints and light/calm \nwinds have supported some patchy fog since late Monday evening. \nBetween the southward progressing stratocu deck, increased mixing, \nand the subsequent arrival of lower dewpoints, it's expected that \nfog should gradually dissipate as the morning goes on.  I'll keep a \nmention of patchy fog in the forecast through daybreak but \nconditions should gradually improve. \n\nAll in all, it should be a quiet weather day once the front moves \nthrough. Winds may briefly gust this morning as the BL deepens and \ntaps into the 25-30kt 925mb winds, but this should rapidly diminish \nthrough the day. It'll be cooler today with temps generally in the \nupper 60s (north) to mid 70s (south). The more noticeable cooldown \nwill come tonight. Between clear skies and light/calm winds, look \nfor lows to easily bottom out in the low to mid 40s, if not upper \n30s in the normally cooler/protected areas. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A dry week mid to late week is expected. \nTemperatures will warm to above normal from Wednesday through \nSaturday. The next chance of rain returns this weekend, but most of \nit may hold off until Sunday. \n\nThe dry and cool surface high pressure will move off the Mid-\nAtlantic coast Wednesday and Wednesday night. A dry reinforcing cold \nfront will slip through NC late Thursday and Thursday night. \nTherefore, sunny and dry conditions are expected Wednesday with \nhighs in the upper 70s to lower 80s. As the winds become SW at 10-15 \nmph ahead of the front Thursday, there will be little moisture to \nwork with. Expect mainly sunny skies and warming temperatures into \nthe mid 80s. Record daily highs are in the lower to mid 90s, so they \nare not in play. It will be slightly cooler behind the front Friday \nwith highs around 80. Lows will be mostly in the lower to mid 50s \nthis week, warmest Friday morning (near 60 in some locations). \n\nThe next chance of showers appears to be over the weekend. There \ncontinues to be quite a bit of model spread with the potential Gulf \nto SE United States system, with the EC ensemble continued to be \npreferred, which may obviously change with time. As of right now, \nthe EC depicts higher rain chances have moved 6-12 hours later than \npreviously forecast. The period between Saturday night through \nMonday morning appear to be when the most significant rain will \nfall.",
